{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And they will listen to your voice.</b> The reason He said <b>your voice</b> is to inform him that they will believe and accept from him, even though he will not tell them His name. Therefore, if He had said ״וְשָׁמְעוּ לִדְבָרֶיךָ״ [and they will listen to your words] or ״אֵלֶיךָ״ [to you], it could still be understood that the reason for their acceptance is on account of the things stated by him. But by His saying <b>your voice</b>, note that a voice is limited in that it proceeds from the person emitting it, not from another, unlike words, which could be said to be spoken by another and are the words of another. Therefore He said <b>your voice</b> — meaning, when he emits his voice, the voice of a herald, they will accept it from him. And with this He removed any impediment to their faith — not the question of the Name, and needless to say signs and wonders." "<b>YHWH, Elohim of the Hebrews.</b> Behold, the Master, YHWH of Hosts, was cunning in saying to him that He is not willing that His name be called except upon the Hebrews, and He did not make known this great name except to the Hebrews. And the intention is so that there would be no opening for Pharaoh to say ״I do not know״ and so forth — for surely HaShem Himself said ״Elohim of the Hebrews,״ but you do not realize that you are greatly despised, and how would a man of the field recognize precious gems?" "<b>Has happened upon us.</b> Meaning, lest one think that they would be accustomed to this and that it would be so the next day and continually in this manner. It is also possible that he would say to them afterward that they should persist in the matter; therefore they said that this thing is not ongoing, nor is it a regular occurrence, but an incidental event — and that is his saying <b>has happened upon us</b>:" "<b>And now let us go.</b> His saying <b>and now</b> means that this is something they must do immediately, without any delay of time, for their Elohim did not fix a time. Or it may intend that this is not a continuing matter but only for the moment, as we explained regarding ״has happened upon us,״ and the intention in this follows the same reason that He said <b>let us go a journey of three days</b>." "<b>Three days.</b> And the reason for the things He said so is because He said shortly after ״וְשָׁאֲלָה אִשָּׁה מִשְּׁכֶנְתָּהּ״ [and a woman shall ask of her neighbor] and so forth, and if they did not say thus, there would be no opportunity to ask of them and borrow from them vessels of silver and so forth and to go out with great property. And with this the mind is at rest as to why HaShem commanded the asking of silver and so forth from the neighbors at an early time, and did not wait until the time of the arrival of His good word to bring them out of the land of Egypt and say it to them there — all the more so since those things there, in their place and in their time, were said on the day before they went out. And if so, what need was there to say them twelve months earlier?" "And it would appear possible to say that this was to strengthen their hearts regarding the truth of the redemption, but this is not the principal point. According to our approach, the explanation blossoms forth (כפתור ופרח), for HaShem needed to tell them the reason that is the surrounding rationale — that they should say ״נֵלְכָה דֶּרֶךְ שְׁלֹשֶׁת יָמִים״ etc., for through this ״וְשָׁאֲלָה אִשָּׁה״ etc. — because their intention was to return. And the word ״וְעַתָּה״ will be explained further below." "<b>But still</b>, one must give heart: after all, why would HaShem command things that are not consonant with ethics — to deceive them, whether regarding the departure or regarding the borrowing of their belongings? And the truth is that the entire matter is an act of justice, for since they had enslaved Israel, they were obligated to pay them the wages of a hired laborer — and go and learn from the response of an Israelite man to the Egyptians (Sanhedrin 91a) when they demanded from them all the vessels they had borrowed. But why would HaShem do the matter through subterfuge, for heaven forbid, is His hand not capable of redeeming Israel against their will and extracting their wealth before their very eyes?" "<b>And it would appear</b> to say that HaShem intended through this to mislead them, so that they would pursue after them, to be made heavy upon Pharaoh etc. — and without the borrowing, and likewise without their traveling this way with intent to return, the Egyptians would not have pursued after them. And see what we have written in parashat Beshalach on the verse ״וַיֵּהָפֵךְ לְבַב פַּרְעֹה״ etc. (Exodus 14:5)." "<b>And you will find</b> a sign for our words, for they said, of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 14:3), that during the days of darkness Israel uncovered the hiding places of the Egyptians and knew their treasuries and their precious possessions and their goods, and on the day they asked for them the Egyptians were unable to deny it. Master of Abraham — and why did they not take them then, without understanding anything? Rather, it is certain that HaShem intended that they should act in this order, so as to pursue after them as mentioned and be made heavy upon them." "And regarding the matter itself — that they took their wealth — there is no prohibition in this for a person to save what is his from a robber, and go and learn from Jacob, who used cunning to save his property from the hands of Laban the Aramean, and go and learn from the episode of the three Sages (Yoma 83a) who rescued their deposit from the wife of Kidon through subterfuge, and there is no prohibition in this. And the prophet says (Psalms 18:27): ״וְעִם עִקֵּשׁ״ etc." "And what stands before us is a cleaner and clearer matter, for I have examined all the things said in this episode and there is in them nothing false, for they did not mention in their expression any pretext at all. And also, from the standpoint of the sanctity of the Elohim of Israel, He did not wish to attribute these words to Himself — rather, it is the children of Israel who would speak thus. Therefore He said the word <b>וְעַתָּה</b> — meaning to say that these are their own words, not the words of the Most High. For if He had not said ״וְעַתָּה,״ the intention would have been along these lines: ״נִקְרָה עָלֵינוּ וְאָמַר נֵלְכָה״ etc. And likewise you will find in Moses' words with Pharaoh — he said to him (Exodus 5:1): ״שַׁלַּח אֶת עַמִּי וְיָחֹגּוּ לִי בַּמִּדְבָּר״ — he did not mention a time limit; but Israel said to him (Exodus 5:3): ״נֵלְכָה נָּא דֶּרֶךְ״ etc. And although in their words there he did not say ״וְעַתָּה,״ he relied on the first verse. And I have understood in words of pleasantness in His saying ״שַׁלַּח אֶת עַמִּי וְיָחֹגּוּ לִי בַּמִּדְבָּר״ that all His words are justice and truth, for the generation that came out of Egypt did not celebrate festivals except in the wilderness, for there their bodies fell, and HaShem brought their children into the land. And although those too came out of Egypt, even so they are not reckoned except the adults who served and sacrificed, and they are from twenty years old and upward, and with this all the words of HaShem are truth and justice. Also the request for silver etc. — there is in it nothing false, for it came to them by way of a request, except that they held it back on account of their claim that they had outstanding against them for the wages of a hireling, that they enslaved them and built many buildings, and when they would come to a reckoning they would audit the account and the Egyptians would pay them what was owed to them." "<b>וְאַחַת</b> — I asked one question that stood before me: what compelled the matter to be so, that the Egyptians would pursue after the children of Israel? And why would HaShem not bring heaviness upon Pharaoh and all his people and bring the Nile flooding upon them by the very thing in which they acted presumptuously, and there HaShem would drown Pharaoh and all his people, also the women and the little children, and there would have been no need for all that is said, and He would bring out Israel with silver and gold against their will — or that they would take everything during the seven days of darkness (Exodus Rabbah, ch. 44), for they had light and searched them as we have written nearby. And perhaps HaShem did so for two reasons: the first, that the splitting of the Sea of Reeds and its wonders publicized His greatness, may He be blessed, and His glory fills the world, as is known — which would not have been accomplished in another manner. And a second reason, that HaShem measures measure for measure, and they said, may their memory be for blessing (Sotah 11a), that the Egyptians schemed against Israel to enslave them at first with soft speech and afterward with harshness, and the Judge of justice measured with the very same measure — for at first with soft speech: ״נֵלְכָה שְׁלֹשֶׁת יָמִים,״ ״הַשְׁאִילוּנוּ כַּסְפְּכֶם שִׂמְלוֹתֵיכֶם״ etc., and afterward with harshness, and in any case the Master devised it so that no word of falsehood would issue even from the mouth of Israel, as is remembered (Micah 7:20): ״תִּתֵּן אֱמֶת לְיַעֲקֹב.״"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {5 "The phrase כפתור ופרח ('capitals and blossoms' or 'knob and flower') is a characteristic Or HaChaim expression signaling the unfolding of an interpretation; translated here idiomatically while preserving the floral imagery.", 8 "מָארֵי דְּאַבְרָהָם ('Master of Abraham') appears to be an Aramaic exclamation or epithet; rendered literally as it stands.", 9 "The reference to 'the wife of Kidon' follows the standard version of the Talmudic anecdote in Yoma 83a; the name appears in some witnesses as כידר and in others as כידון."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:58.139351134Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}